  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 315 PM CST Mon Nov 2 2026\n\nCurrently, long wave upper level trough over the central CONUS\nwith embedded lows over eastern North Dakota and the Southern\nPlains. Associated sfc trough over eastern South Dakota extends\nnorth-northeast into western Minnesota. Widespread rain \ncontinues over most areas of western and central North Dakota, \nbut has mainly diminished over the northwest outside of a few \nsprinkles. This trend will continue from northwest to southeast \nfor the rest of today and tonight, as the main forcing shifts to\nthe east. Thus far have received over an inch of rainfall in \nparts of west central North Dakota, and especially in the \nsoutheast/James River Valley where an inch to over an inch and a\nquarter has been observed. Still on track to maybe seeing \nupwards to 2 inches of rain across parts of the James Valley \nthrough tonight/Tue daytime. \n\nOn the backside of the aforementioned sfc trough, northerly\nwinds have increased and will continue to be gusty into the\nevening hours. Strongest winds still expected far southwest and\nfar south central, where the Wind Advisory remains in effect\ntill 06Z tonight. \n\nSurface high pressures builds into the western Dakotas on\nTuesday, along with a quasi-omega ridge forming over the\nNorthern Rockies, and the Southern Plains upper low lifting\nnorth-northeast to the Upper Mississippi Valley region. This may\nlinger wrap around showers over my southeast for Tuesday and\nmaybe even for Tuesday night and Wednesday as it is projected to\nstall over Minnesota. Clearing over at least the western half \nof the region Tue and Wed. Cooler/near to slightly below normal\nhighs forecast for Tue (40s), then slowly warming on Wed (mid\n40s to lower 50s). \n\nWeak ridging builds into the central CONUS for the later period\nof the week, maintaining mainly dry weather and temperatures\nmoderating to a several degrees above normal by the end of the\nweek (low/mid 50s for Friday forecast). Thereafter, ensembles\nshow a progressive upper level pattern thus higher level of\nuncertainty in the forecast.",
